use ./utils ▪ codemsg

# every source kode accepts and turns into javascript that node cannot parse.
#
# found by six strategies, none of which knew what it was looking for:
# enumerating every token in every position, matrices of keyword against
# operand and operator against operand, the token fuzzer from fuzz.kode
# over many seeds, and mutation fuzzing - deleting spans out of the 4135
# snippets in this repo and seeing what the compiler made of the wreckage.
# ~93.000 sources in all. None of them raised an internal error; every one
# of them raised nothing at all.
#
# each line says what the compiler SHOULD say. they are all '???' because
# nobody has decided yet, which is the point of the file - it is the list.
#
# when one starts passing, move it to error.kode: that file is for messages
# that are settled, this one is for messages that are owed.
#
# the first line is the rule these were collected under, not a guarantee.
# a word that renders as an ordinary identifier is not javascript node
# cannot parse, and a good number of these never were - see 'a keyword with
# no operand' below. the harness is what matters: a case stays here until
# the compiler says something about it, or until someone decides it never
# should.

▸ error_todo

    ▸ a token on its own

        codemsg '\\' '???'
        codemsg '■' '???'
        codemsg '▪' '???'
        codemsg '▸' '???'
        codemsg '◆' '???'
        codemsg '○' '???'
        codemsg '●' '???'
        codemsg '⮐' '???'
        codemsg '->' '???'
        codemsg '=>' '???'

    ▸ a keyword with no operand

        # ten of these are settled and live in error.kode now: else catch
        # finally throw super extends when in await typeof. each is a
        # javascript reserved word or renders as one, so a bare one is
        # always broken.
        #
        # the rest are not compiler bugs and this section was wrong about
        # them. kode uses abs, clamp, copy, dbg, empty, first, from, last,
        # lpad, min, max, noon, of, rpad, trim and valid as ordinary
        # variable names - {new: new} and {delete: delete} are what
        # object.kode says they should compile to - so a word that renders
        # as a plain identifier is a name, and the compiler cannot tell a
        # forgotten call from a variable. 'abs' on its own compiles to the
        # identifier abs, which node parses and then throws on at runtime.
        # not a message the compiler owes.
        #
        # break and continue are the other two: what they are missing is a
        # loop, not an operand, and kode.kode:278 leans on a bare continue
        # standing on its own.
        #
        # do and case are not kode keywords at all. the lexer takes them
        # for names and the renderer hands node a reserved word, so they
        # belong with 'a reserved word used as a name' below.

        codemsg 'break' '???'
        codemsg 'continue' '???'
        codemsg 'do' '???'
        codemsg 'case' '???'

    ▸ a keyword on its own line

        codemsg 'x = 1\no f' '???'
        codemsg 'x = 1\ncase' '???'
        codemsg 'x = 1\nfrom' '???'

